    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d580-a1">

                <pb facs="0747" n="461" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg461"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grey</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the American
                        Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Foreign Office</hi>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1916-10-10">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 10, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Your Excellency</hi>: I have had the honour of
                    receiving Mr. Laughlin’s note of the 3d
                    instant (No. 241) in which he makes a communication under instructions from your
                    Government with respect to the Maritime Rights Order in Council, 1916.</p>
                <p>I would point out that, if the rules cited in the order in council are not deemed
                    by the United States Government to be in accordance with international law, they
                    should be challenged in the prize court.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Maurice de Bunsen</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
